<column class="doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">What's given</p>
  <h1>Elements</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    There are 48 elements and you already know most of them. That is the whole
    design: a model reaches for a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> when it isn't sure,
    so the component library is made of tags it is already fluent in. There is
    no library to import, no library to choose, and nothing to route around.
  </p>

  <h2>Half of them are the browser's</h2>
  <p>
    A dialog is <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>. A disclosure is
    <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>. A menu rides <code>[popover]</code> and the
    top layer. These ship <strong>zero JavaScript</strong> — they're the native
    element plus a stylesheet, so you get the keyboard handling, the focus trap,
    the ARIA, and the top-layer stacking from the platform, correct by default.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.native}></sui-codeblock>
  <p class="mono small">
    button · checkbox · details · dialog · dropzone · fieldset · hr · input ·
    meter · progress · prose · select · skeleton · status-dot · table · textarea
  </p>

  <h2>The rest compile away</h2>
  <p>
    Where the platform has no element — a combobox, a toast region, a
    breadcrumb — SUI defines one. These are written in <code>.sui</code> like
    anything else, and the compiler <em>inlines them</em>: the tag becomes plain
    markup plus a <code>use:</code> action, and no custom element is registered
    at runtime. You write a component; you ship markup.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.language}></sui-codeblock>
  <p class="mono small">
    alert · avatar · badge · breadcrumb · card · chip · collapsible · combobox ·
    context-menu · dropdown · empty-state · error-boundary · error-state · field ·
    hover-card · icon · label · menu-item · nav-top · page · pagination · popover ·
    radio · radio-group · scroll-area · separator · slider · spinner · switch ·
    tab · tab-list · tab-panel · tabs · theme-picker · toast · toast-region ·
    toggle · toggle-group · tooltip
  </p>

  <h2>A variant is a prop, not a component</h2>
  <p>
    This is where most libraries leak decisions. A sheet is not a new component;
    it is a dialog that arrives from the side. An alert dialog is not a new
    component; it is a dialog with a role. Collapsing those into props is what
    keeps the surface at 48 instead of 90 — and it means the model cannot pick
    the wrong one, because there is only one.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.variant}></sui-codeblock>

  <h2>Layout: the axis is the name</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;row&gt;</code> is the x-axis, <code>&lt;column&gt;</code> is the
    y-axis, <code>&lt;stack&gt;</code> is the z-axis, <code>&lt;grid&gt;</code>
    is two dimensions. There is no <code>flex-direction</code> to get backwards
    and no debate about whether a stack is vertical.
  </p>
  <sui-codeblock code={code.layout}></sui-codeblock>

  <p class="callout">
    <strong>The library is very nearly free.</strong> A screen built from twelve
    of these — combobox, tabs, field, tooltip, the lot, with the keyboard
    handling and the ARIA — bundles to <strong>8.5&nbsp;kB gzipped</strong>. The
    same markup written as inert <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>s, with no behavior at
    all, is 7.2&nbsp;kB. Twelve working components cost
    <strong>1.3&nbsp;kB</strong>, because the skins are CSS and the elements
    compile away.
  </p>
</column>
